Under the federal Stored Communications Act, 18 U.S.C. §2703(f), an electronic-communications provider must preserve records/evidence in its possession upon the request of a governmental entity while the government pursues a court order or other legal process. The initial preservation period is 90 days, renewable for another 90 daysnmental entity while the government pursues a court order or other legal process. The initial preservation period is 90 days, renewable for another 90 days

A preservation request can be useful for anyone experiencing cyber harassment, cyberstalking, threats, impersonation, repeated anonymous contact, or other potentially criminal online conduct when the identity behind an account is unknown or important electronic evidence could disappear before an investigation is completed. Instead of immediately requiring the platform to turn records over, law enforcement can ask a service such as TikTok to preserve specified existing account records and evidence while investigators pursue whatever subpoena, warrant, court order, or other legal process would be required to actually obtain them. Federal law provides for preservation for 90 days, renewable for another 90 days, and TikTok has a formal law-enforcement procedure implementing preservation requests. This can be particularly valuable with anonymous or pseudonymous accounts because potentially relevant records—such as account information, login/IP information, interaction records, comments, or other available data—can be protected from routine deletion while the legal and investigative questions are worked out. A preservation request doesn't establish that a crime occurred, identify the perpetrator, or automatically give police access to the records; its purpose is essentially to prevent potentially important electronic evidence from disappearing while those questions are investigated.
